---
name: engine
description: "Run a named engine — unified dispatch with intent-contract pre-flight per ADR-038"
user-invocable: true
argument-hint: "<engine-id-or-alias> [target] [--just-run | /engine!!] [--mode <mode>] [--success-shape <shape>] [--stretch] [--retry run-<id>]"
---

# /engine — Unified Engine Dispatch

Routes engine invocations through an intent-contract pre-flight (ADR-038 Layer 3), then a token-budget gate (ADR-037 Decision #5 / WS-272), then the specialized engine's own multi-phase execution prose. Aliases (e.g., `/eng`, `/design`) are routed via `engines/registry.yaml`. Pedagogy: see `docs/adrs/ADR-038.md` (a dedicated engine-pedagogy guide is planned — WS-278).

## Output Shape

**Engine-run arc:** `<frame-pre-flight | gate-checked | dispatching | researching | synthesizing | findings-filed>` — `<one-clause status>` (e.g., "design-audit run-002, contract-cnt-1234, phase 4 of 6, 3 findings drafted").

`<Delta line: what this invocation did — frame outcome (recorded/bypassed/abandoned), gate status, phases N–M completed, F findings filed, W work items.>`

`<Remainder: per-phase progress + findings table — Severity / Title / Linked WS — sorted severity-first. Cite run_id AND contract_id explicitly.>`

### Why this engine ran
`<Value-rationale: cite the program the engine serves, the contract's mode + scope_ceiling + stretch values, the cadence/protocol that triggered it, or the captured concern that prompted it. If --just-run was used, cite the bypass_reason.>`

**Do next:** Single-line action — `Run /next to compose a sprint covering the new findings` (or `Continue engine run` mid-phase).

## Step 1: Parse + alias resolution

Parse `$ARGUMENTS` for `<engine-id-or-alias> [target] [--just-run | /engine!!] [--mode X] [--success-shape Y] [--stretch] [--retry run-<id>]`. Read `engines/registry.yaml` `aliases:` map; if `<engine-id-or-alias>` matches an alias key, resolve to the canonical id. If no match in aliases, treat as canonical id; if absent from `engines:` map, list available + exit.

## Step 1b: Prior runs on this target (WS-702) — MANDATORY, including `--just-run`

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-runs.js find --target "<target>" --engine <id>
```

Render the result to the founder before framing anything. Two lines is enough:
*"`business-engine` ran on this area 5 days ago — run-027, 3 findings, output in `runs/run-027/`. Reuse it, or is this a fresh look?"*

**This never blocks.** It reports and the run proceeds — the founder decides whether prior work makes this run unnecessary, narrower, or unchanged.

**It runs before Step 2, not after**, because a prior run should inform the contract — scope_ceiling and success_shape are different questions when 80k tokens of research already exist.

**`--just-run` skips Step 2 but NOT this step.** A hurried run is exactly when re-derivation happens, so the escape valve that bypasses the contract does not bypass the lookup.

**Why this exists.** On 2026-08-22 a ServeYourNote session re-derived pricing research from the open web. It searched `queue/`, `ops/financials/` and `system/decisions.md`, found DEC-011 and no engine output, and concluded none existed. `runs-index.yaml` had recorded the run the whole time — every file that referenced the index was a *writer*, so nothing could answer "has an engine already looked at this?". The index was not missing; the query was. Engine runs are the most expensive artifact CWOS produces, and that one was paid for twice.

**Reading the output honestly:**
- A run with `status: abandoned` is **unharvested work, not absent work** — its findings may exist only in artifacts that were never sealed. These sort first.
- A match reporting `0 file(s)` on disk is the run-004 shape: sealed, but its analysis may exist only in a lost transcript. Do not treat it as reusable without opening the directory.
- **Zero matches is not proof that no prior work exists.** The command prints what it searched — index path, run count, files read, terms, match floor. Read that line before concluding. Widen with `--min-score 0.3` or broader terms before spending tokens on a re-derivation (FAIL-018 / INV-088).

## Step 2: Intent-contract pre-flight (ADR-038 Layer 3)

If `--just-run` (or `/engine!!`) is present, **skip** to Step 4 after emitting:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-event.js append engine_intent_bypassed --track T7:engines --tag /engine --payload '{"type":"engine_intent_bypassed","engine":"<id>","target":"<target>","bypass_reason":"founder-explicit","composed_by":"cli-deterministic"}'
```

Otherwise, run pre-flight:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-frame.js compose --engine <id> --target <target> [--stretch]
```

Output is JSON with `contract`, `confidences`, `prompts_needed`, and (when readiness=defer) `readiness_human`. Render to founder:
- If `prompts_needed` is empty AND `readiness === 'ready'`: one-screen confirm summary.
- If `prompts_needed` is non-empty: prompt ONLY those fields, **strictly one at a time — never a 4-question form**. The single-question discipline is enforced by a script gate (WS-433), not just this prose. For each field prompt (ignore `*_warning:` entries — those are informational, no ack needed): ask the founder, **WAIT** for their answer, then emit the ack before moving to the next field:
  ```bash
  node kit/scripts/cwos-frame.js ack-field --engine <id> --field <fieldname>
  ```
  `ack-field` rejects more than one field per call, so you cannot batch. `confirm` (below) will REFUSE the contract unless every prompted field has its own ack — emitting all four acks back-to-back without prompting between them defeats the purpose and is the exact drift this gate exists to stop. To override in a genuine edge case, the founder passes `--force-preflight "<reason ≥20 chars>"` to confirm.
- If `readiness === 'defer'`: render the `readiness_human` block as-is — DO NOT surface the raw `readiness_reason` string (per FIND-127 / WS-291; raw machine strings violate P2 at the moment of decision). Format:
  ```
  Engine deferred. Reasons:
    - <readiness_human.bullets[0]>
    - <readiness_human.bullets[1]>
    ...

  Options:
    1. <readiness_human.options[0].label> — <options[0].outcome>
    2. <readiness_human.options[1].label> — <options[1].outcome>
       <if options[1].override_cost === 'high': also print options[1].override_warning>
    3. <readiness_human.options[2].label> — <options[2].outcome>
  ```
  Bullets carry their own suggested next action (after `→`); print verbatim. Founder picks an option; option 2 routes to `--just-run` re-invocation.

On founder confirmation, write the **full compose envelope** (the `compose` JSON with any founder edits applied to `contract`) to a temp file — keep the `prompts_needed` array intact so the pre-flight gate (WS-433) can see which fields required an ack — then:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-frame.js confirm --contract-file <tmp>
```

This emits `engine_intent_recorded` with the final contract + revisions array. Confirm runs the WS-433 pre-flight gate: if any field in `prompts_needed` lacks its `ack-field` event it exits non-zero — go back and ack each field one at a time (or, for an edge case, re-run with `--force-preflight "<reason>"`). If founder cancels mid-prompt, emit `engine_intent_abandoned` with `fields_touched` + `elapsed_ms` (friction signal).

Confirm also runs the WS-472 one-contract-per-run guard (ADR-038 Decision #6 — the contract is immutable once confirmed): if a contract for the same `(engine, target)` is still open — no `engine_run_completed` since, and less than 6h old — it exits 2 and names the open `contract_id`. If that run actually finished, close it (`cwos-engine-complete.js --run-id <run-NNN>`) rather than working around the guard. To deliberately supersede an open contract, re-run with `--amend "<rationale ≥20 chars>"`; that emits `engine_contract_amended` and marks the replacement, which is what keeps the amendment visible to INV-090 instead of looking like a leak.

## Step 3: Token-budget gate (ADR-037 Decision #5 / WS-272)

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-token-budget.js --check
```

Exits 0 (clean) or 1 (regression detected — block). Escape valves don't compose: `--just-run` bypasses Step 2 but still goes through this gate. Founder unblocks via `--override-token-budget "<rationale ≥20 chars>"` (per WS-272).

## Step 4: Load specialized engine

Read the engine's `skill_path` from `engines/registry.yaml`. Load the engine MD file at that path. The contract is in the **confirm envelope produced in Step 3** (`cwos-frame.js confirm` output: `contract_id`, `event_id`, and the full `contract`) — the specialized engine's Setup phase reads those fields directly from that envelope. It MUST NOT reverse-scan `.claude/workstream/events/*.jsonl` to re-derive the contract (that re-reads data already handed to it and is the run's largest avoidable token cost); the raw-log scan is a bounded fallback only, used when the confirm envelope is absent (e.g. `--retry` resume or a pre-ADR-038 kit).

Specialized engines own their own multi-phase execution prose (Setup → Parallel Research → Cross-Critique → Synthesis → Backlog → Briefing). Find the engine MD file and follow its instructions. Apply contract fields:
- `mode` shapes the output type (decide → comparison; build-best → committed implementation; mockup → low-fi sketch; explore → adjacent possibilities)
- `stretch=true` invites the engine to question loaded AS-N tags + constraints (NEVER re-read `system/` files — question what's already in the envelope)
- `scope_ceiling` is the out-of-bounds bound the engine must respect
- `success_shape` is the structured target the briefing phase MUST honor
- `readiness_reason` (if defer was overridden) is logged in the run manifest

## Step 4.5: Initialize manifest (WS-305)

Before per-phase execution, write the canonical pre-artifact:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-engine-manifest.js init \
  --run-id run-<NNN> --engine <engine-id> --target <program-or-focus> \
  --contract-id <cnt-id> --contract-event-id <ev-id> \
  --mode <mode> --stretch <true|false> --readiness <ready|defer> \
  --success-shape "<from contract>" --scope-ceiling "<from contract>" \
  --founder <handle> \
  --declared-phases "phase_0,phase_1,phase_2,phase_3,phase_4,phase_5,phase_6" \
  --declared-agents "<comma-separated persona names from engine's ## Agents section>"
```

The manifest is the engine-run twin of WS-304's protocol-run provenance: `agent-dispatch.md` updates the live punchlist via `cwos-engine-manifest update` after every agent return, and Step 6 closes it with `complete` + `validate`. Schema: `kit/templates/workstream/runs/MANIFEST.md`.

## Step 4.6: Stake a pointer in the target's canonical doc

An engine run has a `target`. Most targets have **one document a human or a later session actually reads** — `pricing-model` has `ops/financials/pricing-model.md`, a program has its `prog-*.yaml`, an ADR has its file. Resolve that document now, at the START of the run, and stamp a pointer into it:

```markdown
<!-- cwos:last-engine-run -->
> **Last engine run:** `run-<NNN>` — `<engine>`, started `<ISO date>`, status `in-progress`.
> Contract, artifacts and findings: `.claude/workstream/runs/run-<NNN>/`
<!-- /cwos:last-engine-run -->
```

Record the resolved path as `target_doc` when you call `cwos-engine-manifest init`. Refresh the block's status at Step 6 (`complete`) or when sealing early. The HTML comments make it idempotent — replace the block between them, never append a second one.

**Why at the start.** The pointer's whole job is to survive a run that does not finish. Written at the end, it is written exactly when it is least likely to happen. ServeYourNote's `ops/financials/pricing-model.md` still said *"Pricing model selected — deferred until after first users onboarded"* on the day an 80k-token pricing engine ran against it; the doc had also gone stale against ADR-011 months earlier. Nothing pointed from the document to the work, so the work was invisible from the only place anyone would look.

If no single canonical doc exists for the target, record `target_doc: null` **and say so in the briefing** — a target with nowhere to write back is a gap worth naming, not a silent pass.

## Step 5: Per-phase execution

Follow the loaded specialized engine's phase prose. Engine briefing phase MUST include a `contract_alignment` block stating which contract fields were honored vs. departed from (with reason for each departure). Per Decision #6, contract is **immutable post-confirm** — engines do not revise mid-run.

### Persistence is not optional (WS-364 / run-004)

**A phase's output is written to disk the moment it is produced, BEFORE the next phase consumes it.** Not at the end of the run. Not "if the run completes."

```bash
# after EVERY agent return / phase output, in this order:
#   1. write the artifact          2. record its path in the punchlist
mkdir -p .claude/workstream/runs/run-<NNN>/artifacts/phase-<N>
#   ...write <name>.md there...
node kit/scripts/cwos-engine-manifest.js update --run-id run-<NNN>   --phase phase_<N> --agent <persona> --artifact-path <path> --status complete
```

**Why this is a hard rule, not a nicety.** ServeYourNote run-004 (`business-engine` on `pricing-model`, 2026-08-22) burned ~80k tokens across two research agents and a cross-critique. Its manifest recorded `artifact_path: null` for both agents while `phase_2_cross_critique` recorded `complete` — the engine cross-critiqued research that had never been written down. The run then stopped before Step 6, so nothing sealed, and `runs-index.yaml` reported it as not existing at all. A later session searched for prior pricing work, was told there was none, and re-derived it from the open web.

The failure was not that the run stopped. Runs stop — context fills, the founder pivots, a session dies. **The failure was that stopping destroyed everything, because nothing durable existed until the very last step.** Write-as-you-go makes an interrupted run a partial harvest instead of a total loss.

Three rules follow from that:

1. **The transcript is not storage.** An analysis that exists only in the conversation is lost the moment the session ends. If it was worth the tokens, it is worth a file.
2. **Never let a later phase consume an artifact the earlier phase did not write.** Cross-critique reads files, not chat history. If the file is missing, the phase is blocked — say so, do not proceed on remembered content.
3. **A load-bearing agent claim is verified before it reaches synthesis.** run-004's top-ranked finding asserted, from a repo-wide grep, that no `application_fee_amount` existed anywhere — `services.py:174` sets it. Same shape as FIND-prog-self-compliance-001. Grade every claim per `evidence-standards.md`; an agent's confident output is **Inferred** until independently checked.

### If the run cannot finish

Do not leave it unsealed. Seal what exists — a partial harvest is recoverable, an unsealed run is not:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-run-summary.js --run-id run-<NNN>   # seals from whatever artifacts exist
```

Then tell the founder plainly what was harvested and what was lost. An abandoned run now indexes as `status: abandoned` with a warning rather than vanishing, but that warning says *unharvested*, not *recovered* — sealing is still yours to do.

## Step 5.5: Finding discipline — the per-run `finding_cap` (WS-443 / FS-7)

Five engine files reference a per-run `finding_cap`; this is its definition. The constitution treats a large undifferentiated finding dump as a failed state (FS-7 fires at ≥15 per run — run-009 emitted 83, run-014 emitted 65, and neither was actionable). The discipline is **prioritize, don't suppress**:

- **Cap: at most 12 findings promoted per run** (i.e. written as `FIND-*.yaml`). At synthesis, rank by severity then impact and promote the top of the ranking. 12 sits deliberately under FS-7's threshold of 15.
- **Criticals are never dropped.** If critical findings alone exceed the cap, promote them all and say so in the briefing — that overflow is itself the headline finding.
- **The long tail is recorded, not discarded**: everything ranked below the cap gets one line each in the run's synthesis artifact (`runs/<run-id>/artifacts/phase-3/synthesis.md`, section `## Below the cap`). Silent signal loss would be its own constitutional violation; a recorded-but-unpromoted tail is neither noise nor loss.
- **`blindspot: true` findings bypass the cap** (they exist precisely because normal passes miss them — blind-spot protocols surface their full set even on noisy days). Honor `waived_blindspots`.
- The FS-7 detector (`cwos-constitutional-audit.js`) counts non-blindspot promoted findings per run over the last 30 days; a run that respects this cap cannot trip it.

## Step 6: Finalize + reconcile

After all phases complete:

```bash
node kit/scripts/cwos-engine-manifest.js complete --run-id run-<NNN> \
  --findings-raw <N> --findings-after <N> --work-items <N> \
  --mode-status <honored|departed|violated> --mode-note "<one-liner>" \
  --stretch-status <…> --stretch-note "<…>" \
  --success-status <…> --success-note "<…>" \
  --scope-status <…> --scope-note "<…>"

node kit/scripts/cwos-reconcile.js --quiet
node kit/scripts/cwos-engine-complete.js --run-id run-<NNN>
```

`cwos-engine-manifest complete` writes the post-artifact (contract_alignment block + counts) to `manifest.yaml`. `cwos-reconcile` then rebuilds queue/findings/sprint indexes after the engine wrote its outputs. `cwos-engine-complete` calls `cwos-engine-manifest validate` first (WS-305 gate); if validation fails, no `engine_run_completed` event is emitted and a `FIND-MFST-*` finding is written under `prog-engine-reliability`. On clean validation it emits the `engine_run_completed` event (WS-263 / WS-302) tying `run_id` back to the contract_id — required so the verifier (`cwos-engine-contract-verify`) binds via the canonical event rather than falling through to all-time heuristic lookup. The emit step is idempotent (skips if a `run_id` event already exists) and derives `engine_id` + `program_id` from the most recent `engine_intent_recorded` event when not passed explicitly.

**Conversion bridge (WS-489 / DEC-039).** On clean completion, `cwos-engine-complete` also auto-promotes this run's open CRITICAL/HIGH findings into queue items marked `review: pending` (its JSON output carries the report under `promotion`; the durable copy is the `promotion:` block in `runs/<run-id>/summary.yaml`). Report it to the founder in business terms:

- "Promoted N of M findings to your work queue (conversion NN%): WS-NNN (FIND-NNN, critical) — <title>, … They're awaiting your yes/no in `/next`."
- MEDIUM findings (`promotion.medium`) are briefing-only, never queued — list them: "Worth improving, not queued: FIND-NNN — <title>."
- If `promotion.errors` is non-empty or `promotion.ok` is false, surface it and heal with: `node kit/scripts/cwos-engine-complete.js promote-findings --run-id run-<NNN>` (dedup-safe to re-run; also the path for runs that cannot close normally).

## Step 7: Edge cases

| Situation | What to do |
|-----------|------------|
| Founder runs `/eng-engine` (legacy direct invocation) | Treat the engine name as both id-and-alias-of-itself; route through Steps 2-6 normally |
| `--retry run-<id>` flag | Skip Steps 2-3 (contract + budget already recorded for original run); resume Step 4-6 from the retry point per the original engine's prose |
| Engine MD file missing | Surface "engine MD not found at <path> — registry may be stale" + exit |
| Pre-flight `prompts_needed` includes ≥3 fields | Suggest `--just-run` if founder explicitly wants to bypass framing this once (per Decision #3 discoverability) |
| Defer outcome with no override | Engine DOES NOT run; report defer reason + suggested action; no token cost |

## Prohibited Reads

After Step 4 loads the specialized engine MD + contract envelope, the AI MUST NOT re-read these (the CLI envelope captured them): `engines/registry.yaml`, the contract artifact, `engines/standard/*.md` (except the loaded one), `engines/library/*/*.md` (except the loaded one), `kit/scripts/core/engine-readiness-registry.yaml`. Re-reads defeat ADR-037's token-savings goal AND ADR-038's contract-binding intent. INV-cli-envelope-consumed-completely (WS-271, scope-expanded by WS-277) routes excess Read tool calls to prog-kit-quality.

## CLI-absent fallback

If `kit/scripts/cwos-frame.js` is missing (older kit): "cwos-frame.js not found — run `/fleet-update` to install ADR-038 Layer 3 contract pre-flight. Without it, engines run with default mode=explore, readiness=ready, stretch=false (no contract event emitted)."

## Pedagogy

Why the contract? Why these 5 fields? See `docs/adrs/ADR-038.md` (a dedicated engine-pedagogy guide is planned — WS-278).

## Shadow-event envelope

`node kit/scripts/cwos-event.js append command_completed --track T7:engines --tag /engine --payload '{"command":"/engine"}'` — non-fatal; never gate output on it.
